  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between active substance approval and product authorisation under the EU BPR?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The EU Biocidal Products Regulation operates a two-tier system. First, the active substance — the ingredient responsible for the biocidal action, such as a quaternary ammonium compound, hydrogen peroxide, or ethanol above a defined concentration — must be approved at EU level and listed in Annex I of the BPR or in a delegated act. Second, the finished biocidal product formulation (which contains the active substance along with co-formulants) must then obtain its own product authorisation before it can be placed on the EU market. You cannot sell a biocidal product simply because its active substance is approved; the complete product itself must be separately authorised. This two-step structure means that even a reformulation of an existing product — changing co-formulants, concentration, or intended use — typically requires a new or amended product authorisation." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which product type applies to my disinfectant under the EU BPR?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The BPR defines 22 product types (PTs) across four main groups. For disinfectants and sanitisers, the most relevant are: PT1 (human hygiene biocidal products — products applied directly to human skin or scalp for disinfection, such as hand sanitisers and surgical scrubs); PT2 (disinfectants and algaecides not intended for direct application to humans or animals — surface disinfectants, instrument disinfectants, and general area disinfectants); PT3 (veterinary hygiene biocidal products); and PT4 (food and feed area disinfectants used on surfaces in food processing). The classification matters because each PT has different efficacy testing requirements, active substance lists, and authorisation pathways. Misclassifying a product — for example, registering a surface disinfectant as PT1 when it is applied to surfaces rather than skin — is a common error that results in authorisation refusal or post-market enforcement action." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do I need BPR authorisation for a disinfectant I am already selling in Europe?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Whether existing products require immediate full BPR authorisation depends on the active substance review status. Products whose active substances are still under the EU review programme — a transitional period inherited from Directive 98/8/EC — can continue to be placed on the market under national rules while the review is ongoing, provided the product was already marketed before the review commenced. However, once a review programme decision is made and an active substance is approved (or not approved), the transitional period ends and full BPR product authorisation becomes mandatory within defined transition timelines. Products containing active substances that have been approved and for which the transitional period has expired must have valid product authorisation. Checking the active substance status via the ECHA R4BP database and the Review Programme regulation is the essential first step for any manufacturer in this situation." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is Union authorisation and is my disinfectant product eligible?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Union authorisation is a centralised EU-wide authorisation granted by the European Commission following evaluation by ECHA, which allows the authorised biocidal product to be placed on the market in all EU member states under a single authorisation number. It is available for standard biocidal products (Article 42 BPR), low-risk biocidal products (Article 25), and products containing only new active substances. Not all disinfectants are eligible — products with uses that require member state-specific conditions, such as language-specific labelling requirements or national public health restrictions, may not qualify for the standard Union authorisation route and must use national authorisation with mutual recognition instead. For manufacturers seeking EU-wide market access, Union authorisation is the most efficient route where eligibility is met." }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What efficacy tests are required for a PT2 surface disinfectant?",
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "For PT2 disinfectants intended for surface disinfection, the core efficacy testing framework uses the European Committee for Standardisation (CEN) quantitative suspension test series: EN 13697 for bactericidal and fungicidal activity on non-porous surfaces, EN 16615 for bactericidal and yeasticidal activity under practical conditions on non-porous surfaces (4-field test), and EN 17272 for airborne disinfection. Where virucidal claims are made, EN 14476 (quantitative suspension test) is required. Sporicidal claims require EN 13704. All tests must be conducted at the claimed use concentration, contact time, and temperature, and must meet the standard pass criteria (typically a 5 log reduction for bacteria, 4 log for fungi). Tests must be performed by accredited laboratories and the data submitted as part of the authorisation dossier to the competent authority." }
